		<tr>
			<td bgcolor="#FFFFCC"><p style="line-height: 150%">
The following is a copy of an email that I sent to
<a href="https://president.byu.edu/directory/kevin-j-worthen">Kevin Worthen</a>,
who was president of BYU when I &quot;retired&quot; in 2020. President Worthen was a very
good man, and I don't blame him at all for not getting involved at this late
stage in the process, nor did I really expect him to. I just wanted him to
understand how the system had failed so badly in the previous 3-4 years, and to
suggest to him a couple of simple ways that this might be fixed, so that what
had happened to me would never again happen to anyone else at BYU. I'm not sure
if anyone ever followed up on either of these suggestions. </td>
		</tr>
		<tr>
			<td>
			<p style="line-height: 125%"><br>
			From:
			<a href="https://www.mark-davies.org/">Mark Davies</a>, Department of Linguistics</p>
			<p style="line-height: 125%">
			To: President Kevin Worthen, Brigham Young University<p style="line-height: 125%">
			Re. Scott Miller, College of Humanities<p style="line-height: 125%">
			Date: 18 May 2020<p style="line-height: 125%">
			<font color="#808080">(Note: additions to my letter for this web
			page are in gray
			below, and web links added as well)<br>
&nbsp;</font><hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<br>
			Dear President Worthen,<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			I know that you are very busy, and I appreciate your taking the time
			to read this. I would not have gone “all the way to the top” if
			things could have been resolved with other administrators like Brad Neiger or
			Shane Reese, but you will see a discussion of this below.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			By way of introduction, I believe that you and I talked after the
			meeting where I received the
			<a href="https://science.byu.edu/description-of-university-awards#:~:text=Creative Works Award,and distribution nationally or internationally.">Creative Works Award</a> in 2017, as well
			as after the University Conference where I received the Karl G. Maeser
			<a href="https://avp.byu.edu/fac-dev/awards/faculty-award-descriptions">Research and Creative Arts Award</a> in 2015. You may also know
			me as the creator of the (erstwhile)
			<a href="https://magazine.byu.edu/article/big-lang-at-byu/">BYU Corpora</a>
			<font color="#808080">(now </font>
			<a href="https://www.English-Corpora.org"><font color="#808080">
			English-Corpora.org</font></a><font color="#808080">)</font>, which
			your colleagues at the Law School (like
			<a href="https://law.byu.edu/faculty/d-gordon-smith/">Gordon
			Smith</a>, <a href="https://law.byu.edu/faculty/thomas-r-lee/">Tom Lee</a>,
			and
			<a href="https://wheatley.byu.edu/james-c-phillips-new-constitutional-government-director">
			James Phillips</a>)
			<a href="https://www.deseret.com/2011/3/14/20179097/u-s-supreme-court-uses-corpus-created-by-byu-professor-mark-davies">have been using</a> for
			several years.<br>
&nbsp;<table border="1" cellpadding="7" style="border-collapse: collapse" width="100%" bordercolor="#BFDFFF" cellspacing="0">
				<tr>
					<td bgcolor="#EEF7FF">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			This summer I am <b>retiring</b> at the relatively <b>young age of 57</b>, because
			of what I believe is a <b>hostile work environment</b> in the College of
			Humanities, and specifically actions that <b>
			<a href="https://hum.byu.edu/directory/scott-miller">Scott Miller</a></b> has carried out
			against me since May 2016.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Retiring at the age of 57 means that I will <b>lose out on more than
			a million dollars</b> of income and future pension benefits.
			Hopefully this shows how bad things have been during the last few
			years – that I would be willing to give up this amount of money and
			leave my position at BYU, which had always been my “dream job”. I
			would not be doing this if the issues discussed here were simply “fevered
			imaginations” on my part.</td>
				</tr>
			</table>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<br>
			Very briefly, since 2016 <b>
			<a href="https://hum.byu.edu/directory/scott-miller">Scott Miller</a></b> has done the following:<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			1. From 2016-2017, he carried out what was for me a fairly traumatic, (financially)
			<b>high-stakes, 10 month audit</b> of my corpus activities. Scott
			simply would not back down once he had initiated this
			university-wide audit, even after
			my previous chairs and deans wrote and told him his concerns were completely
			unfounded. In the end, BYU (via
			<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brent_W._Webb">Brent Webb</a><font color="#808080">,
			Academic Vice President in 2017</font>) confirmed that his
			concerns were in fact unfounded, but Scott never apologized for
			masterminding this audit.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			2. The audit raised questions in my mind about the <b>long-term
			viability of the corpora</b> at BYU. I emailed Scott about this
			several times, and he finally confirmed
			that I would have no access to the corpora after I left BYU, which
			meant in essence that <b>they would disappear</b> at that point. This in
			spite of the fact that the corpora were the most widely-used
			web-based resource by people outside of BYU (and especially in
			China); see <a href="https://www.english-corpora.org/atbyu.asp">
			https://www.english-corpora.org/atbyu.asp</a>.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			3. In November 2018, I informed Scott that I would be taking the
			corpora off-campus, which was the only way to keep them viable
			long-term. Scott then forced me to sign a <b>Conflict Management Plan</b>,
			in order for me to keep my position at BYU. (<font color="#808080">This
			agreement stated that I would continue to do teaching and research
			even if the corpora were off-campus. This was insulting, because I
			had been in the <b> <a href="humanities-byu.asp">top 1-2% of scholarly productivity</a></b> in the college
			during the previous 10-15 years and had received multiple
			university-level research awards -- even as I worked on the same
			corpora, which were at that time on-campus)</font>. Apparently I am the only
			person in the College of Humanities who has ever had to do this, and
			this in spite of the fact that Scott knows of several others in the
			college who are involved in off-campus activities.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			4. By December 2018 I had negotiated a <b>licensing agreement</b> with BYU
			to take the corpora off-campus. Everyone else had signed the
			agreement, but <b>Scott</b> alone <b>refused to sign</b>. He kept it in his office
			for almost three weeks, and complained to
			<a href="https://innovation.byu.edu/directory/giovanni-tata">Giovanni Tata</a>
			<font color="#808080">(who has always been very helpful to me)
			</font>that it
			“gave away too much to Mark Davies”. This in spite of the fact that
			it was the “standard” BYU licensing agreement; no special
			concessions were made for me.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			5. On 2 January 2019 – at about 9:30 AM -- and within 5-10 minutes of “being convinced” to sign
			the agreement (by Giovanni Tata and others) – Scott emailed
			<a href="https://cwo.byu.edu/">Creative
			Works</a> and (without informing me) told them to <b>&quot;move&quot; more than $10,000 in income</b> from
			<a href="https://www.english-corpora.org/academic_license.asp">academic licenses</a> for the corpora (from
			<a href="https://www.english-corpora.org/academic_licenses_current.asp">universities</a>
			like Oxford, Stanford, and Princeton) to another college account
			-- where it could not be used for the purposes for which these
			universities had contributed the money.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			6. After I found out on 14 February 2019 what had happened, Scott Miller and
			<a href="https://hum.byu.edu/directory/jared-christensen">Jared Christensen</a> (College Controller) purposely tried to <b>conceal
			their </b>previous <b>actions</b>. In the end, they claimed that the explicit
			request to Creative Works on January 2 -- to move the
			$10,000 from other universities to an account where it could never be used for its intended
			purpose -- had “all been a simple mistake”.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			7. I contacted <b>Brad Neiger</b> in April 2019 to explain what had
			happened. He was only willing to meet with me for about 10 minutes,
			but he did meet with Scott Miller for more than an hour the
			following week. In May 2019, <b>Scott Miller</b>
			had a 1.5 hour meeting with the chair and associate chairs in my
			department, in which (among many other actionable comments), he
			mentioned that he was tired of having me at BYU, and that he <b>wanted
			me gone</b> as soon as possible.<br>
&nbsp;<table border="1" cellpadding="7" style="border-collapse: collapse" width="100%" bordercolor="#BFDFFF" cellspacing="0">
				<tr>
					<td bgcolor="#EEF7FF">
					<p align="justify" style="line-height: 150%">8. At that meeting
					in May 2019 with members of the Department of Linguistics,
					Scott Miller also explained that from that point forward,
			<b>I could no longer receive any research support</b> (travel funds, professional
			development leaves <font color="#808080">(sabbaticals)</font>, research assistants, etc) for any research that might in any way
			relate to the (now) off-campus corpora. Because virtually all of
					<a href="humanities-byu.asp">my
			research</a> is related to these corpora, this means that I am now cut
			off from <i>any funding</i> for research. I am almost completely hamstrung
			in my activities, and there is now <b>no option other than &quot;retirement&quot;</b>.</td>
				</tr>
			</table>
			<p align="justify" style="line-height: 150%">&nbsp;</p>
			<hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">Scott will deny all of these points, but he is not telling the
			truth. I have emails and witnesses for every point mentioned above.
			Scott has almost nothing to support his claims (other than his
			“authority” as dean).</p>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Scott will say that these are just tired old accusations from a
			paranoid, disgruntled, &quot;prima donna&quot;. However, it is strange that no
			other problems like these ever happened with previous deans under
			whom I worked at BYU. (For example, you
			can ask <a href="https://avp.byu.edu/directory/john-r-rosenburg">John Rosenberg</a>, who was
			dean of the College of Humanities from 2005-2015, if he ever had any
			issues with me. We got along extremely well, as I did with other
			deans and department chairs). The problems only started when Scott Miller became dean
			of the College of Humanities in
			2015.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			What has made this even more difficult is that almost <b>no one</b> from
			the ASB <font color="#808080">(BYU Administrative Building; i.e.
			BYU's central administration)</font>
			<b>has been willing to help</b>. (I should say that
			<a href="https://avp.byu.edu/directory/larry-l-howell">Larry Howell</a> is
			the one exception; he has been great. <font color="#808080">But he
			wasn't really in a position to help resolve problems with Scott
			Miller.</font>)<p style="line-height: 150%">
			1. <b>
			<a href="https://avp.byu.edu/directory/brad-l-neiger">Brad Neiger</a></b>
			--<font color="#808080"> who as head of &quot;</font><a href="https://avp.byu.edu/fac-relations"><font color="#808080">Faculty Relations</font></a><font color="#808080">&quot; is the person assigned to
			help resolve issues like this</font> -- showed no
			interest at all in seeing any evidence to support my claims. He did meet
			with Scott in April 2019; Scott convinced him that I was a “troublemaker”, and
			that was that. Brad Neiger also explicitly refused to pursue any
			action after April 2019, even when #7-8 above occurred.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			2. When I mentioned the
			“misplaced” $10,000 to <b>
			<a href="https://assessmentandplanning.byu.edu/directory/david-paul">David Paul</a></b>
			<font color="#808080">(University Treasurer)</font> in a
			meeting on 28 March 2019, he told me bluntly to not “air my dirty
			laundry” to him, and he refused to get involved. I thought this was
			a bit strange, since BYU was in essence &quot;misappropriating&quot; money
			that had been entrusted to them by other universities throughout the
			world.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			3. The one person who definitely could have helped to resolve things
			was <b>
			<a href="https://president.byu.edu/biography">Shane Reese</a></b>
			(<font color="#808080">Academic Vice President from 2019-2023</font>),
			but he did absolutely nothing to help. In spite of emails that I
			sent to him from October 2019 until January 2020, where I explained
			in some detail #1-8 above, he never met with me or was willing to
			get involved in any way. Shane Reese reappointed Scott Miller to a
			second term as dean in January 2020, without ever having asked for
			feedback from any of the 200 faculty in the College of Humanities.
			This went against the standard practice at BYU, and against the
			promises that Shane had made to me personally in late 2019. Finally,
			Shane Reese cancelled
			appointments with the two associate
			chairs in my department, who wanted to talk to him in February 2019 about other
			“issues” with Scott Miller, and who could have confirmed #1-8 above.
			Shane Reese <b>utterly failed to protect faculty</b> in the College
			of Humanities from Scott Miller.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			In other colleges at BYU (such as the College of Education), there are
			annual reviews of the deans and associate deans. In the College of
			Humanities, there is no meaningful review – ever. Scott Miller knows
			this, and he knows that this means that he can create an extremely
			abusive work environment – with no repercussions whatsoever.<br>
&nbsp;<hr>
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<b><br>
			When I came to BYU</b> in 2003 (after twelve years as a professor at Illinois
			State University), <b>I was so excited</b> to contribute to the wonderful
			environment here at BYU. And for the next twelve years (2003-2015),
			it was everything I had hoped for. But the last five years (during
			the time that Scott Miller has been chair) have been a nightmare.
			Not the least because of the almost complete lack of help or support
			from anyone at the ASB <font color="#808080">(BYU central
			administration)</font>.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<b>I now leave BYU completely disillusioned and demoralized</b>.
			(So maybe Scott Miller is right about the “disgruntled” part after
			all.) I never thought that my career would end this way. But at
			least I am fulfilling Scott’s hope – mentioned to the chair and
			associate chairs from my department in May 2019 – that he could get
			me to leave BYU as soon as possible.<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Although I will be gone from BYU after July 2020, as I leave I would
			like to suggest the following:<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			1) There be some meaningful annual review of the dean of the College
			of Humanities, and<p style="line-height: 150%">
			2) There be some review of how the central administration at BYU (especially Faculty
			Relations and the Office of the Academic Vice President) fails to address concerns from faculty, and just “closes
			ranks” to protect administrators such as college deans.
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Hopefully these minimal efforts can help to <b>ensure that what has
			happened to me won’t ever happen to anyone else</b> at BYU.<br>
&nbsp;<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Sincerely,<p style="line-height: 150%">
			<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Mark Davies<p style="line-height: 150%">
			Department of Linguistics<p>
&nbsp;</td>
		</tr>
	</table>
